  "preamble": "Raising of the near-open front vowel [æ] to the close-mid front vowel [e]. It is documented in Persian, where the word-final front vowel of Early New Persian was raised to [e] (xāna > xâne 'house'). In Old Frisian and in the Old English dialects other than West Saxon, the long ǣ that Anglo-Frisian brightening had made from West Germanic ā was raised to ē ('second fronting'): West Saxon slǣpan 'to sleep' corresponds to Anglian slēpan and Old Frisian slēpa.",
